+ *    notice, this list of conditions and the following disclaimer.
+ * 2. Redistributions in binary form must reproduce the above copyright
+ *    notice, this list of conditions and the following disclaimer in the
+ *    documentation and/or other materials provided with the distribution.
+ *
+ * THIS SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED BY THE COPYRIGHT HOLDERS AND CONTRIBUTORS
+ * "AS IS" AND ANY EXPRESS OR IMPLIED WARRANTIES, INCLUDING, BUT NOT
+ * LIMITED TO, THE IMPLIED WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY AND FITNESS FOR
+ * A PARTICULAR PURPOSE ARE DISCLAIMED. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE COPYRIGHT
+ * HOLDER OR CONTRIBUTORS BE LIABLE FOR ANY DIRECT, INDIRECT, INCIDENTAL,
+ * SPECIAL, EXEMPLARY, OR CONSEQUENTIAL DAMAGES (INCLUDING, BUT NOT
+ * LIMITED TO, PROCUREMENT OF SUBSTITUTE GOODS OR SERVICES; LOSS OF USE,
+ * DATA, OR PROFITS; OR BUSINESS INTERRUPTION) HOWEVER CAUSED AND ON ANY
+ * THEORY OF LIABILITY, WHETHER IN CONTRACT, STRICT LIABILITY, OR TORT
+ * (INCLUDING NEGLIGENCE OR OTHERWISE) ARISING IN ANY WAY OUT OF THE USE
+ * OF THIS SOFTWARE, EVEN IF ADVISED OF THE POSSIBILITY OF SUCH DAMAGE.
+ */
+
+/*
+ * MinGW compat shim: minimal hb-icu replacement.
+ *
+ * Modern HarfBuzz releases (>= 12) removed the ICU integration entirely:
+ * there is no libharfbuzz-icu and no <hb-icu.h> header anymore, and
+ * MSYS2/MinGW environments therefore cannot provide them. The GTK port
+ * of WebKit only uses one entry point from that API:
+ *
+ *     hb_icu_script_to_script(UScriptCode)
+ *
+ * This header re-implements it inline on top of ICU's uscript API and
+ * HarfBuzz's hb_script_from_string(), matching the semantics of the
+ * original implementation in harfbuzz/src/hb-icu.cc. Since it is a
+ * static inline function no hb_icu_* symbols are ever referenced, so
+ * linking against plain libharfbuzz + libicuuc is sufficient.
+ */
+
+#ifndef MINGW_COMPAT_HB_ICU_H
+#define MINGW_COMPAT_HB_ICU_H
+
+#include <hb.h>
+#include <unicode/utypes.h>
+#include <unicode/uscript.h>
+
+#ifdef __cplusplus
+extern "C" {
+#endif
+
+static inline hb_script_t
+hb_icu_script_to_script(UScriptCode script)
+{
+    if (script == USCRIPT_INHERITED)
+        return HB_SCRIPT_INHERITED;
+    if (script == USCRIPT_COMMON)
+        return HB_SCRIPT_COMMON;
+
+    /* uscript_getShortName() returns the 4-letter ISO 15924 code (e.g. "Latn"),
+     * or NULL for invalid script codes. */
+    const char* tag = uscript_getShortName(script);
+    if (!tag || !*tag)
+        return HB_SCRIPT_UNKNOWN;
+
+    return hb_script_from_string(tag, -1);
+}
+
+#ifdef __cplusplus
+}
+#endif
+
+#endif /* MINGW_COMPAT_HB_ICU_H */
diff --git a/Source/cmake/FindHarfBuzz.cmake b/Source/cmake/FindHarfBuzz.cmake
index 6dd9b8b..49af958 100644
--- a/Source/cmake/FindHarfBuzz.cmake
+++ b/Source/cmake/FindHarfBuzz.cmake
@@ -124,6 +124,21 @@ if ("ICU" IN_LIST HarfBuzz_FIND_COMPONENTS)
         else ()
            list(APPEND HarfBuzz_LIBS_FOUND "ICU (optional): ${HarfBuzz_ICU_LIBRARY}")
         endif ()
+    elseif (WIN32)
+        # Modern HarfBuzz releases no longer ship the ICU integration
+        # (no harfbuzz-icu library, no hb-icu.h), so MSYS2/MinGW systems
+        # cannot provide it. WebKit only uses hb_icu_script_to_script(),
+        # which the mingw-compat shim re-implements inline on top of
+        # libharfbuzz and libicuuc -- both already linked. Point the ICU
+        # component at the core library so the HarfBuzz::ICU imported
+        # target still exists for the FreeType/Skia CMake modules.
+        set(HarfBuzz_ICU_LIBRARY "${HarfBuzz_LIBRARY}")
+        set(HarfBuzz_ICU_INCLUDE_DIR "${HarfBuzz_INCLUDE_DIR}")
+        if (HarfBuzz_FIND_REQUIRED_ICU)
+            list(APPEND HarfBuzz_LIBS_FOUND "ICU (via mingw-compat shim): ${HarfBuzz_ICU_LIBRARY}")
+        else ()
+            list(APPEND HarfBuzz_LIBS_FOUND "ICU (optional, via mingw-compat shim): ${HarfBuzz_ICU_LIBRARY}")
+        endif ()
     else ()
         if (HarfBuzz_FIND_REQUIRED_ICU)
            set(_HarfBuzz_REQUIRED_LIBS_FOUND OFF)
From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Taozuhong <taozuhong@hotmail.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 07:22:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] MinGW: Skia FreeType backend and GTK font manager

- ThirdParty/skia: build the FreeType/Fontconfig branch under MinGW
  (the release tarball lacks all four skia/src/utils/win/ files needed
  by the DirectWrite backend); file IO via SkOSFile_win.cpp instead of
  the sys/mman.h-requiring posix variant; SK_TYPEFACE_FACTORY=FREETYPE.
- FontCacheSkia.cpp: the GTK platform uses SkFontMgr_New_FontConfig
  (same shape as Linux GTK) instead of DirectWrite.

  Update 26 (fixing the round-26 UCRT64 Skia -Wformat warnings, first
  hit at SkAssert.h:141 via SkLog.h:35 while compiling SkSVGText.cpp):
  SK_PRINTF_LIKE expands to format(printf, ...) and MinGW gcc checks
  the plain printf archetype against the MSVCRT (ms_printf), which does
  not know C99 length modifiers, so every SkLog/SK_ABORT call site
  taking size_t warned "unknown conversion type character 'z' in
  format" plus "too many arguments for format" (9 warnings per TU on
  every Skia TU including SkAssert.h). The runtime side is fine on all
  three environments: __USE_MINGW_ANSI_STDIO=1 (PKGBUILD CFLAGS) routes
  printf-family calls through the C99-conformant __mingw_*
  implementations and the UCRT printf handles %zu natively. The
  attribute now maps to __gnu_printf__ for MinGW gcc on _UCRT or
  __USE_MINGW_ANSI_STDIO targets, mirroring mingw-w64's own
  __MINGW_PRINTF_FORMAT mapping and the PAS_FORMAT_PRINTF mapping
  already established in 0006; clang (which checks the plain archetype
  as C99 and produced no CI warnings) and non-MinGW hosts keep the
  default. The SkAssert.h TU goes from 9 warnings to 0.

  Update 18 (fixing one UCRT64 and one CLANG64 Skia failure):
  include/private/SkAttributes.h maps SK_ALWAYS_INLINE to
  __forceinline whenever SK_BUILD_FOR_WIN is set, and both MinGW
  compilers trigger that branch via _WIN32. MinGW GCC only survives
  the keyword when a CRT header happens to be included first - the
  mingw-w64 _mingw.h defines __forceinline as a macro and libstdc++
  pulls one in before SkAssert.h:191 - but clang64's libc++ headers
  pull no CRT header on that path, so the token reached the parser
  and SkAssert.h:191 died with "unknown type name '__forceinline'".
  The keyword is now reserved to real MSVC-family compilers
  (SK_BUILD_FOR_WIN && _MSC_VER), matching the guard SkFeatures.h
  already applies to SK_RESTRICT; MinGW compilers fall back to the
  portable SK_ATTRIBUTE(always_inline) inline form.
  src/core/SkRasterPipeline.h enables SK_HAS_MUSTTAIL whenever
  __has_cpp_attribute(clang::musttail) fires, and GCC 15 understands
  [[clang::musttail]]. The Win64 ABI passes Skia's 16-byte vector
  values by invisible reference, so GCC rejects every highp stage
  tail call with "cannot tail-call: argument must be passed by
  copying" (first hit at sse3::set_base_pointer while compiling
  SkOpts.cpp; 20 errors before -fmax-errors cut it off). The flag now
  also requires !(__MINGW32__ && !__clang__): MinGW clang folds the
  same tail calls successfully (verified against the emitted objects,
  since clang passes the vectors by value there) and keeps them,
  while MinGW GCC compiles the stages as regular calls, which is also
  how MSVC builds behave.

  Update 20 (fixing one CLANG64 and one CLANGARM64 Skia failure):
  src/ports/SkFontHost_FreeType.cpp includes <dlfcn.h> whenever the
  default SK_FREETYPE_MINIMUM_RUNTIME_VERSION carries the
  SK_FREETYPE_DLOPEN flag (SkFontHost_FreeType.cpp:91), and mingw-w64
  does not ship <dlfcn.h>, so both CLANG64 and CLANGARM64 died on the
  first FreeType translation unit with "fatal error: 'dlfcn.h' file
  not found". The dl*() symbols are not referenced anywhere in the
  file - that include is the flag's only remaining consumer - so the
  include is now guarded with !defined(_WIN32). Windows builds simply
  use the FreeType library they link against, which is what the
  dlopen fallback would have handed them anyway.
---
