From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Taozuhong <taozuhong@hotmail.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 07:22:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] MinGW: libpas/bmalloc and compatibility shims

Compatibility shims:
- err.h shim for the GLib/unix code paths that use err()/errx();
- unifdef format fixes (eval%ld for LP64).
- pas_utils.h/err.h format attributes mapped per mingw-w64 stdio.h
  (MinGW gcc -> __gnu_printf__, clang/host unchanged).
libpas/bmalloc:
- pas_thread.h: drop the MSVC pthread emulation in favour of the
  system <pthread.h> under MinGW; exclude the emulation .c; guard
  scavenger/status_reporter thread main signatures.
- Explicit <windows.h> includes for PAS_OS(WINDOWS) files that use
  WinAPI without any Windows header (upstream relies on prefix
  headers): pas_committed_pages_vector.c, pas_page_malloc.c,
  pas_scavenger.c, pas_probabilistic_guard_malloc_allocator.c,
  pas_lock.c (futex WaitOnAddress/INFINITE - mingw-w64 synchapi.h
  does not pull winbase.h), pas_status_reporter.c (Sleep).
- __PAS_API expands to nothing under __MINGW32__: PE/COFF has no
  visibility semantics and MinGW GCC warns on every annotated
  declaration; libpas links as a static library.
- pas_large_sharing_pool.h: keep [[clang::preferred_type]] only
  under __clang__ (GCC ignores it on PE and warns per TU).
- bmalloc/CMakeLists.txt: link synchronization on MINGW
  (WaitOnAddress/WakeByAddress* live in libsynchronization.a);
  bmalloc is static so the requirement propagates to the final
  JavaScriptCore link.
- Update 15 (xdgmime fails to compile on MinGW, [6131]/8552 on ucrt64):
  the mingw-w64 CRT ships neither <fnmatch.h> nor <netinet/in.h>. New
  shims: fnmatch.h implements the classic glob subset (*, ?, [...],
  backslash escaping) inline - the WebKit consumers (xdgmime glob
  matching, GStreamer pattern checks) always pass flags = 0, the
  standard FNM_* constants are defined for completeness and the
  matcher is function-tested against 25 POSIX-semantics cases;
  netinet/in.h provides inline ntohl/ntohs/htonl/htons byte-order
  conversions instead of reusing winsock2.h, whose declarations are
  dllimport and would drag the ws2_32 import library into every final
  link of the static xdgmime archive. All eight xdgmime sources compile
  and link with the shims (cross-gcc, zero extra libraries).

- Update 16 (ucrt64 [6103]/8552, xdgmimecache.c compiled as C): the
  sys/mman.h shim was written in C++ (reinterpret_cast, static_cast,
  nullptr) and is now pulled into the C translation unit
  xdgmimecache.c because HAVE_MMAP is force-enabled under MinGW since
  update 12. The shim is rewritten with C-style casts, NULL and a
  <stdint.h> include so the identical header serves C and C++
  consumers; verified against all eight xdgmime C sources with the
  cross compiler and against the WTF C++ mmap consumers.

- Update 25 (fixing the round-25 CLANG64/CLANGARM64 WebCore
  unified-source failures): mingw-compat gains a <libintl.h> wrapper.
  gettext's libintl.h (MSYS2 gettext-runtime 1.0, lines 588-590)
  unconditionally installs the object-like macro chain
  "printf -> libintl_printf -> __printf__" whenever it is included -
  glib's i18n machinery (gi18n-lib.h) drags it into many WebCore
  unified sources after the PCH has already declared WTF::PrintStream.
  The preprocessor knows nothing about member access, so a later header
  calling the member compiles as "out.__printf__(...)" and dies with
  "no member named '__printf__' in 'WTF::PrintStream'" - CI hit it at
  JSC ValueProfile.h:115/118 (out.printf(": ")/out.printf(", "),
  reached via UnlinkedMetadataTable.h) in UnifiedSource-html-9/10/12,
  and the same hazard covers the out.vprintf(...) call sites in
  ScopedPrintStream.h, DataLog.cpp, LinkBuffer.cpp and Integrity.cpp.
  The wrapper #include_next'es the real header and then #undefs exactly
  that *printf-family redirect set (printf, vprintf, fprintf, vfprintf,
  sprintf, vsprintf, snprintf, vsnprintf, asprintf, vasprintf), which
  is behavior-neutral on this build: with -D__USE_MINGW_ANSI_STDIO=1
  (always set by the PKGBUILD) the CRT printf family is already
  C99-conformant - the libintl macros ultimately wrap the very same
  CRT symbols on MinGW, because libintl_printf is defined as the
  __printf__ builtin alias - while the gettext/dgettext/... family,
  setlocale/newlocale and libintl.h's "namespace std" using-declarations
  stay untouched; WebKit routes translatable positional-format strings
  (LocalizedStrings.cpp WEB_UI_FORMAT_STRING) through GLib's
  g_strdup_vprintf, which never sees the macros. Verified in the sandbox
  against the real MSYS2 clang64/clangarm64 gettext-runtime 1.0
  libintl.h: a TU declaring PrintStream before libintl.h reproduces the
  CI error set line-for-line (same notes pointing at libintl.h:588/590),
  and the same TU with the wrapper searched first compiles clean -
  including printf("%zu") and dgettext() use and object codegen - on
  both targets.
---
